# DBUG1 (stream S2-bodies, worker W12, EQ fuzz row 0x0081e440) - a virtual call on a by-value member that GCC resolves
# at compile time (docs/PATCHES.md "DBUG1").

@@ DBUG1 src/staging/blk_00800000_od.cpp 0x0081e440
# x86 0x0081e440 (/Od, cdecl, plain `ret`): `mov ecx,[ebp+8]; add ecx,0x18` (this = the embedded member at +0x18),
# then `mov eax,[ebp+8]; mov edx,[eax+0x18]` (the member's vptr, read at run time) and `mov eax,[edx+0x14]; call eax`
# (slot 5), and the result is pushed for the call of 0x0081e460. So the exe makes a real vtable call whose target
# the object's constructor chose. The decomp spells the call `object->embedded.vf14()` on a member held by value:
# GCC treats a by-value member as having its static type and calls the stand-in body directly (`mov w0,#0;
# b 0x0081e460` natively), never reading the vptr. MSVC does not do that for a member reached through a pointer, so
# MSVC keeps the original text (byte-identical by construction); every other compiler calls through a local pointer
# to the member, which GCC does not fold (the vptr is loaded and the slot called, as the exe does).
<<<
    LoadAndCloneModel(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(object->embedded.vf14()));
===
#ifdef _MSC_VER
    LoadAndCloneModel(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(object->embedded.vf14()));
#else
    FUN_0081e440_Embedded* embedded = &object->embedded;
    LoadAndCloneModel(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(embedded->vf14()));
#endif
>>>
